<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand Magellan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1480–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Fernão de Magalhães</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand Magellan was a Portuguese navigator who led a Spanish expedition from 1519 to find a western route to the Spice Islands. He passed the strait that bears his name and crossed the Pacific, reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1521, where he met local rulers and was killed at Mactan. His surviving ships loaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> at Tidore, and one completed the first circumnavigation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan.jpg" alt="Ferdinand Magellan" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Magellan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Magellan was born in northern Portugal, possibly around 1480. His father, Pedro de Magalhães, was a minor member of the Portuguese nobility and mayor of the town, and his mother was Alda de Mezquita. He was brought up as a page of Queen Eleanor, consort of King John II, and in 1495 he entered the service of Manuel I.</p><p>In March 1505 he enlisted in the fleet sent to host <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> as the first viceroy of Portuguese India, and he remained there eight years. He took part in the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a> in 1506, where he was wounded, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> in 1509. In 1511, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a>, he and his friend Francisco Serrão took part in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. Serrão went on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, and his letters to Magellan later gave decisive information about the spice-producing territories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>King Manuel I repeatedly rejected Magellan's proposals for expeditions to the Spice Islands. Magellan then offered the project to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles I of Spain</a>, who approved it. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494 Portugal was to control the eastern routes around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, so Magellan proposed a southwestern passage around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. He was given command of the five-ship Armada of Molucca and sailed in August 1519 with about 270 men. King Manuel did all he could to disrupt the preparations, and a Portuguese fleet sent after him failed to capture him.</p><p>The fleet crossed the Atlantic and went south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>. Despite storms and mutinies, it passed through the Strait of Magellan into the sea Magellan renamed the Mar Pacifico, landed at Guam and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Magellan converted as many as 2,200 locals to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-humabon">Rajah Humabon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapulapu">Lapulapu</a>, the leader of Mactan, resisted. On the morning of 27 April 1521 Magellan sailed to Mactan with a small force and was killed in the battle that followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a> and Ginés de Mafra left accounts of the death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapulapu">Lapulapu</a> refused a ransom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron for the body. Under Juan Sebastián Elcano the expedition reached the Spice Islands, and the ship Elcano commanded returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in September 1522, completing the first circumnavigation of Earth. In the aftermath Magellan was widely discredited in Spain and Portugal. Some in Portugal regarded him as a traitor for having sailed for Spain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand Magellan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antonio Pigafetta, who visited with the survivors of <b>Magellan</b>'s expedition in 1521, reported a large city built over the water, which he put at some 25,000 households, and a court where the ruler received visitors with elephants dressed in silk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 6 March 1521 <b>Ferdinand Magellan</b>’s men reached the islands after crossing the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/culture/chamorro-people">Chamorro people · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After crossing the Pacific, <b>Ferdinand Magellan</b>'s ships sighted Samar and landed at Homonhon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-16">16 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Father Pedro de Valderrama of <b>Ferdinand Magellan</b>'s expedition conducted the first documented Catholic Mass in the Philippines on Easter Sunday, on a shore that Antonio Pigafetta's journals call Mazaua.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-31">31 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After 38 days navigating the strait at the tip of South America, <b>Ferdinand Magellan</b>'s three remaining ships reached the open ocean.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinand Magellan</b> left Sanlúcar de Barrameda with five ships and about 270 men.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-20">20 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ponte da Barca, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Mactan, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.8,-8.42&amp;year=1506" aria-label="Ponte da Barca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4345 675 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand Magellan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4795 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4795 945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.8,-8.42&amp;year=1506">What was here in 1506? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
